--TEST--
The hole appears exactly once on a `|>` right side and nowhere else at all, and a callable on the right side is named as the PHP habit it is (`rule:expressions/pipeline-hole-once`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/the-pipeline-operator
// `rule:expressions/pipeline-hole-once`: exactly one hole is what makes the
// substitution total — no temporary, no double evaluation, and a tree
// identical to the nested spelling's. Each line below breaks that one way.
string $s = "hi";

// No `$_`, so the piped value has nowhere to go.
echo $s |> Core\Str::upper("shout"), "\n";

// Two holes on one right side: the second has nothing left to take.
echo $s |> Core\Str::replace($_, $_, "x"), "\n";

// A method reference on the right is the PHP 8.5 habit, and the diagnostic says
// so rather than leaving the reader to discover that `|>` substitutes here.
echo $s |> Core\Str::upper(...), "\n";

// A hole outside a right side has no meaning at all.
echo $_, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0129]: the right side of `|>` needs the hole `$_`
%A
error[E0130]: `$_` may appear exactly once on the right side of a `|>`
%A
error[E0129]: the right side of `|>` needs the hole `$_`
%A
   = note: PHP 8.5's `|>` applies a callable; Novis's substitutes `$_` at compile time
%A
error[E0131]: `$_` is the pipeline hole and has no meaning here
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
